    # 51..65: Step-by-step reasoning / problem solving (15)
    step_data = [
        ("Step-by-step cotton processing flow", "Outline the sequential steps involved in converting raw cotton growing in a farm field into a wearable cotton T-shirt.",
         "Student says: 'Pick cotton, sew it with a needle.'",
         "1. Harvesting mature cotton bolls. 2. Ginning (separating seeds from fibres). 3. Carding/Cleaning (combing fibres parallel). 4. Spinning (twisting fibres into yarn). 5. Knitting or Weaving (interlocking yarns into fabric). 6. Dyeing and Bleaching. 7. Stitching/Tailoring into a T-shirt.",
         "Textile manufacturing follows a rigorous multi-stage industrial transformation from agricultural harvesting to mechanical spinning, textile formation, and garment tailoring.",
         "Step 1: Harvesting: farm workers pick fluffy white cotton bolls from mature Gossypium bushes.\nStep 2: Ginning: bolls pass through ginning machines to remove embedded black cotton seeds.\nStep 3: Carding: wire-toothed cylinders disentangle fibres and remove trash, forming loose slivers.\nStep 4: Spinning: spinning frames draw out and twist slivers into strong, continuous cotton yarn.\nStep 5: Fabric formation: circular knitting machines interloop the cotton yarn into stretchy jersey fabric.\nStep 6: Finishing: fabric is washed, bleached, dyed with colour, and pre-shrunk.\nStep 7: Cut & Sew: fabric is cut into pattern pieces and stitched on sewing machines into a finished T-shirt.",
         "Skipping essential intermediate stages like ginning, carding, and spinning.",
         "Baking bread from wheat: grow wheat -> harvest grain -> mill into flour -> knead dough -> bake loaf.",
         "None", "In which step are the cotton seeds separated from the soft lint fibres?",
         "Ensure students understand the complete agricultural-to-industrial textile pipeline."),

        ("Lab flame test protocol for fibre identification", "Write the step-by-step laboratory procedure to identify whether an unlabelled white fabric thread is cotton, wool, or nylon using a spirit lamp flame.",
         "Student lights the entire garment on fire inside the classroom.",
         "1. Pull a single loose thread from the fabric edge using forceps. 2. Hold the thread with metal forceps near the flame of a spirit lamp. 3. Observe behavior in flame, smell of smoke, and nature of residue upon cooling.",
         "Because different fibres have distinct chemical compositions (cellulose, protein, plastic), observing ignition, smoke odour, and residue provides definitive analytical identification.",
         "Step 1: Extract a single clean yarn (2-3 cm long) from the fabric sample using metal forceps.\nStep 2: Approach the flame slowly: note whether the thread curls away from heat (synthetics) or stays straight (cotton).\nStep 3: Introduce thread edge into the flame: observe if it burns rapidly (cotton) or melts (nylon).\nStep 4: Wave smoke gently towards your nose: identify smell (burning paper = cotton; burning hair = wool/silk; sweet chemical = synthetic).\nStep 5: Extinguish and inspect cool residue: crush residue with metal forceps (soft grey ash = cotton; crushable black bead = wool; hard uncrushable plastic bead = nylon).\nStep 6: Record observations in a laboratory data table.",
         "Touching molten burning synthetic plastic with bare fingers instead of metal forceps.",
         "A detective checking fingerprints at a crime scene: each fibre leaves a unique chemical fingerprint when burned.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Why must you always use metal forceps rather than bare fingers to hold the burning thread?",
         "Drill rigorous laboratory chemical safety and disciplined analytical observation."),

        ("Paper strip weaving model", "Describe step-by-step how a Class 6 student can construct a working model of woven fabric using two different colored sheets of paper.",
         "Student glues two sheets of paper flat on top of each other.",
         "1. Cut parallel slits along a blue paper sheet leaving a border to form the warp. 2. Cut a red paper sheet into separate long strips to form the weft. 3. Weave red strips alternately over and under the blue slits. 4. Paste edges to create a woven checkerboard mat.",
         "This classic pedagogical activity models the exact warp-and-weft interlacing mechanics executed by giant industrial textile looms.",
         "Step 1: Take Sheet A (Blue): fold in half and cut parallel slits 1 cm apart, stopping 2 cm before the edge (acts as warp yarns).\nStep 2: Unfold Sheet A: it now has vertical parallel paper strips held firmly by a solid border frame.\nStep 3: Take Sheet B (Red): cut completely into individual horizontal strips 1 cm wide (acts as weft yarns).\nStep 4: Weave Strip 1: thread over the first blue slit, under the second, over the third, under the fourth, to the end.\nStep 5: Weave Strip 2: alternate the pattern (under the first, over the second, under the third, over the fourth).\nStep 6: Push strips snugly together and secure loose ends with glue, creating a two-colour woven checkerboard fabric.",
         "Failing to alternate the over-and-under pattern on adjacent rows, resulting in non-interlocking strips.",
         "Weaving paper strips is exactly how a loom weaves horizontal weft thread across vertical warp threads.",
         "Physical activity", "What happens if you weave Strip 2 with the exact same over-and-under pattern as Strip 1 without alternating?",
         "Reinforce the alternating mathematical pattern of plain weave."),

        ("Calculating cotton boll to fabric yield", "A farmer harvests 300 kg of raw seeded cotton bolls. During ginning, 65% of the weight is separated as seeds and trash. How many kilograms of clean cotton lint are obtained for spinning?",
         "Student multiplies 300 by 65 and gets 19,500 kg.",
         "105 kg of clean cotton lint is obtained. (Seeds = 65% of 300 kg = 195 kg. Clean lint = 300 kg - 195 kg = 105 kg, or 35% of 300 kg = 105 kg).",
         "Raw cotton bolls consist mostly of heavy, dense seeds. Cotton lint (the fluffy fibre) typically accounts for only about one-third (33-35%) of the harvested boll mass.",
         "Step 1: Identify total raw harvested mass: 300 kg.\nStep 2: Identify percentage of seeds/waste: 65%.\nStep 3: Calculate percentage of usable cotton lint: 100% - 65% = 35%.\nStep 4: Calculate mass of lint: 300 kg × 0.35 = 105 kg.\nStep 5: Verify via subtraction: Seed mass = 300 × 0.65 = 195 kg; Lint mass = 300 - 195 = 105 kg.\nStep 6: Conclude exactly 105 kg of clean spinnable cotton fibre is produced.",
         "Forgetting to subtract seed waste percentage from total mass when calculating usable fibre yield.",
         "Peeling 10 kg of groundnuts gives 7 kg of shells and 3 kg of edible peanuts.",
         "None", "What percentage of the harvested cotton boll mass is typically represented by pure cotton lint?",
         "Teach quantitative agricultural yield calculations for industrial raw materials."),

        ("Jute retting monitoring and extraction", "A jute farmer submerges harvested stalks in a river. Describe the step-by-step process used to determine when retting is complete and how fibres are stripped.",
         "Student leaves stalks in the water for six months until they dissolve completely.",
         "1. Check stalks daily after day 10 by pulling bark with fingers. 2. When fibres separate easily from the woody core, stop retting. 3. Beat stalk bases with wooden mallets. 4. Peel fibres off by hand, rinse in water, and hang on bamboo racks to sun-dry.",
         "Under-retting leaves gummy pectin that makes fibres stiff and unspinnable; over-retting rots and weakens the cellulose fibres. Farmers test stalks empirically by finger-peeling.",
         "Step 1: Submerge bundled stalks horizontally in clean, slow-moving river or pond water, weighted with aquatic plants.\nStep 2: After 10 to 12 days, remove a sample stalk and test by teasing fibres with fingertips.\nStep 3: If fibres separate effortlessly from the inner woody stalk (hurd) from root to tip, retting is perfectly complete.\nStep 4: Farmers stand waist-deep in water, break the bottom of stalks with wooden mallets, and strip the long ribbons of fibre.\nStep 5: Rinse stripped fibres in clean water in swirling motions to wash away slime and decomposed bark.\nStep 6: Squeeze excess water and spread across bamboo racks to sun-dry for 2 to 3 days.",
         "Under-retting or over-retting due to lack of daily biological monitoring.",
         "Boiling potatoes just until a fork slides in smoothly: undercooked is hard, overcooked is mushy soup.",
         "Physical activity", "What causes the gummy tissues between jute fibres to break down during retting?",
         "Explain anaerobic bacterial fermentation in natural retting."),

        ("Comparing water absorbency of different fabrics", "Design a step-by-step classroom experiment to measure and compare the water absorption capacity of equal-sized swatches of cotton, wool, and polyester fabric.",
         "Student guesses without measuring any water.",
         "1. Cut equal 10 cm × 10 cm swatches of cotton, wool, and polyester. 2. Measure dry weights on a balance. 3. Soak all three swatches in 100 mL water for 2 minutes. 4. Hang for 30 seconds to drain dripping water, then weigh wet swatches. 5. Water absorbed = Wet weight - Dry weight.",
         "Controlled scientific experimentation requires identical sample surface areas, identical soaking durations, and quantitative measurement of absorbed water mass.",
         "Step 1: Cut identical 10 cm × 10 cm swatches of 100% Cotton, 100% Wool, and 100% Polyester fabric.\nStep 2: Weigh each dry swatch on a precision digital balance and record: Dry Weight (W1).\nStep 3: Submerge each swatch in a beaker containing 100 mL of room-temperature water for exactly 2 minutes.\nStep 4: Remove with forceps and allow loose water to drip for exactly 30 seconds.\nStep 5: Weigh each saturated swatch and record: Wet Weight (W2).\nStep 6: Calculate absorbed water mass: ΔW = W2 - W1.\nStep 7: Compare absorption ratios (ΔW / W1): Cotton and wool absorb high water mass; polyester absorbs negligible water.",
         "Failing to standardize swatch dimensions or draining time before weighing.",
         "Comparing how much juice three different sponges soak up by weighing them before and after dipping.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which fabric will show the lowest absorbed water mass: cotton, wool, or polyester?",
         "Drill controlled experimental design and quantitative data collection in textile physics."),

        ("Diagnosing fabric pilling and synthetic friction", "A student notices that their new polyester-cotton blend school uniform pants have developed hundreds of tiny fuzzy balls (pills) on the surface after 3 weeks of wear. Explain step-by-step why this happens.",
         "Student thinks tiny caterpillars are chewing on their pants.",
         "1. Everyday friction and rubbing break weak cotton fibres. 2. Unbroken, ultra-strong polyester fibres anchor the broken cotton fuzz to the surface. 3. Ongoing mechanical rubbing rolls the loose fuzz into tight tangled balls called pills.",
         "Pilling occurs on blended fabrics because synthetic fibres are too strong to break away when surface fibres fray, anchoring unsightly fuzzy balls to the cloth.",
         "Step 1: Daily mechanical friction: thighs rubbing together or sitting on rough wooden benches abrades surface fibres.\nStep 2: Fibre breakage: weaker cotton fibres fracture under surface friction, creating loose hairy fuzz.\nStep 3: Anchor effect: adjacent synthetic polyester fibres are exceptionally strong and do not break.\nStep 4: Entanglement: continuous friction rolls the loose cotton fuzz around the anchoring polyester filaments.\nStep 5: Result: persistent, tangled spherical pills that cannot fall off, making the garment look worn.",
         "Assuming fabric fuzz balls are caused by external dirt or insect damage.",
         "Hair tangled around a strong rubber band that cannot be pulled free.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a cross-section of a fabric surface showing a strong synthetic fibre anchoring a tangled fuzz ball.",
         "Teach material mechanics and surface abrasion in blended textiles."),

        ("Designing a cold-weather layering system", "Outline a 3-layer clothing plan for an explorer travelling to high-altitude Leh-Ladakh in winter (-15°C), justifying the fabric selected for each layer.",
         "Student puts on three heavy cotton shirts and a denim jacket.",
         "Base Layer: Thermal polyester/polypropylene (wicks skin sweat to keep dry). Middle Layer: Wool or fleece sweater (traps insulating stagnant air). Outer Layer: Windproof, waterproof nylon shell jacket (blocks freezing winds and snow).",
         "In sub-zero conditions, cotton is dangerous because it holds sweat and freezes. Modern survival layering assigns specific physical functions to each textile layer: wicking, insulation, and wind-proofing.",
         "Step 1: Base Layer (Skin Contact): 100% synthetic thermal underwear (polypropylene/polyester); wicks perspiration away from skin, preventing evaporative chilling.\nStep 2: Middle Layer (Thermal Insulation): thick pure woollen sweater or polyester polar fleece; traps millions of tiny dead air pockets to retain metabolic body heat.\nStep 3: Outer Layer (Weather Barrier): tightly woven nylon jacket coated with breathable membrane; prevents cold wind from penetrating and melting snow from soaking inner layers.\nStep 4: Rule check: verify that zero cotton garments are worn close to skin.",
         "Using heavy cotton garments that retain perspiration and induce fatal hypothermia.",
         "A thermos bottle: inner container stays dry, middle vacuum prevents heat transfer, outer metal case blocks wind.",
         "None", "Why is wearing denim jeans forbidden in extreme sub-zero mountaineering expeditions?",
         "Teach practical thermal physiology and survival gear selection."),

        ("Tracing sericulture yield: Cocoons to silk saree", "A single silkworm cocoon yields approx. 800 metres of fine usable silk filament, but the filament is so thin that it takes approx. 5,000 cocoons to produce enough silk yarn for one traditional silk saree (500 g). Step by step, calculate the average mass of silk yielded per cocoon.",
         "Student guesses a cocoon weighs 5 kilograms.",
         "Each cocoon yields approximately 0.1 grams of pure reeled silk fibre (500 grams ÷ 5,000 cocoons = 0.1 g per cocoon).",
         "Silk filaments are extraordinarily light and fine (only 1 to 2 denier in thickness). It takes thousands of insect cocoons to accumulate enough mass for a single luxury garment.",
         "Step 1: Total silk mass required for one saree: 500 grams.\nStep 2: Total number of cocoons reeled: 5,000 cocoons.\nStep 3: Calculate mass yield per cocoon: 500 g ÷ 5,000 cocoons = 0.10 g (100 milligrams) per cocoon.\nStep 4: Conclude each silkworm secretes only a tenth of a gram of usable silk protein, explaining the high cost of pure silk sarees.",
         "Underestimating the microscopic fineness and lightness of individual insect silk filaments.",
         "Collecting single drops of honey from thousands of flowers to fill one jar of honey.",
         "None", "Why must multiple silk filaments (typically 4 to 8 cocoons) be reeled together simultaneously to make a single strand of silk yarn?",
         "Teach quantitative biological yield and industrial reeling mechanics."),

        ("Testing fabric shrinkage during washing", "A student washes a 100 cm × 100 cm piece of new unwashed cotton cloth in hot water. After drying, it measures 94 cm × 96 cm. Calculate the percentage shrinkage in both length and width step by step.",
         "Student says the cloth shrank by 100%.",
         "Shrinkage in length is 6% (from 100 cm to 94 cm); shrinkage in width is 4% (from 100 cm to 96 cm).",
         "During spinning and loom weaving, yarns are held under high mechanical tension. When submerged in hot water, the stretched fibres relax, swell, and recoil back to their natural coiled state, causing dimensional shrinkage.",
         "Step 1: Initial dimensions: Length (L1) = 100 cm, Width (W1) = 100 cm.\nStep 2: Final dimensions: Length (L2) = 94 cm, Width (W2) = 96 cm.\nStep 3: Calculate length reduction: ΔL = 100 - 94 = 6 cm. Shrinkage % = (6 / 100) × 100 = 6.0%.\nStep 4: Calculate width reduction: ΔW = 100 - 96 = 4 cm. Shrinkage % = (4 / 100) × 100 = 4.0%.\nStep 5: Conclude fabric shrank 6% along the warp (length) and 4% along the weft (width).",
         "Confusing dimensional shrinkage percentage with total fabric destruction.",
         "Stretching a rubber band tight and then releasing it: it snaps back to its relaxed shorter length.",
         "None", "Why do tailors always wash and iron new cotton cloth before cutting and stitching clothes?",
         "Teach practical tailoring science and dimensional fabric relaxation."),

        ("Comparing drying rates of natural vs synthetic fabrics", "Three wet swatches (Cotton, Wool, Polyester) of equal size containing 20 grams of absorbed water each are hung on a clothesline in the sun. Predict and explain the order in which they will dry completely.",
         "Student thinks wool dries first because sheep live outside in the rain.",
         "Order of drying (fastest to slowest): 1. Polyester (fastest, minutes), 2. Cotton (moderate, 1-2 hours), 3. Wool (slowest, several hours).",
         "Polyester fibres are hydrophobic and do not absorb water internally; water sits only on the surface and evaporates quickly. Cotton absorbs water into its lumen and cell walls. Wool absorbs water deep into its protein cortex (up to 30% of its dry weight) without even feeling wet, releasing it very slowly.",
         "Step 1: Analyze fibre affinity for water molecules: Polyester = hydrophobic; Cotton = hydrophilic cellulose; Wool = hygroscopic protein.\nStep 2: Water location in Polyester: surface film only -> rapid air evaporation in minutes.\nStep 3: Water location in Cotton: absorbed inside hollow lumen and cellulose fibrils -> moderate evaporation rate.\nStep 4: Water location in Wool: chemically bound inside keratin protein matrix -> slowest evaporation rate.\nStep 5: Conclude drying speed: Polyester > Cotton > Wool.",
         "Assuming drying rate depends strictly on fabric thickness rather than chemical water affinity.",
         "Drying a smooth plastic plate with a towel takes seconds; drying a thick wet sponge takes hours.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which fabric would be ideal for a quick-drying swimsuit: wool, cotton, or polyester/nylon?",
         "Connect molecular hydrophobicity to everyday laundry drying rates."),

        ("Identifying warp vs weft in a fabric swatch", "Given an unlabelled rectangular scrap of woven cloth with no finished selvage border, outline three diagnostic tests to distinguish the warp yarns from the weft yarns.",
         "Student guesses based on which direction looks prettier.",
         "1. Pull stretch test: warp yarns have less stretch and higher tension; weft yarns stretch slightly more. 2. Yarn strength test: warp yarns are stronger, smoother, and have higher twist to withstand loom beating. 3. Pattern test: stripes usually run in the warp direction.",
         "During weaving, warp yarns must withstand intense mechanical stress from the moving shuttle and harness frames, requiring stronger, tighter spun yarns compared to weft yarns.",
         "Step 1: Stretch test: pull the fabric firmly in both directions. The direction with zero stretch is almost always the warp (held under loom tension).\nStep 2: Yarn unravelling: pull one yarn from each direction. The warp yarn will be smoother, tighter-twisted, and harder to break.\nStep 3: Weft characteristics: weft yarns are often softer, slightly crimped, and have lower twist to provide fabric bulk and softness.\nStep 4: Conclude identification of warp and weft axes.",
         "Assuming warp and weft yarns are identical in spinning twist and strength.",
         "The strong vertical steel pillars in a building carry the main weight; the horizontal drywall studs just fill the space.",
         "Physical activity", "Pull on an old cotton handkerchief along both axes: can you feel which axis has zero stretch?",
         "Teach fabric structural analysis and loom engineering parameters."),

        ("Evaluating tensile strength of sewing threads", "A student tests the breaking strength of cotton, silk, and nylon sewing threads of equal thickness using a spring balance. Predict the relative breaking loads and explain the structural reason.",
         "Student thinks cotton thread is the strongest because it comes from trees.",
         "Nylon thread is the strongest (highest breaking load), Silk is second strongest, and Cotton is the weakest among the three.",
         "Synthetic nylon is an aligned petrochemical polyamide with immense molecular chain length and strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding. Silk is a continuous natural protein filament, whereas cotton is made of short spun staple fibres that slip under high tension.",
         "Step 1: Cotton structure: made of short staple fibres (2-3 cm) twisted together; snaps when fibres slip past each other under tension.\nStep 2: Silk structure: continuous protein filament with natural fibroin tensile strength; significantly stronger than cotton.\nStep 3: Nylon structure: continuous synthetic polyamide polymer chains engineered for extreme tensile resistance; higher strength-to-weight ratio than steel.\nStep 4: Conclude relative breaking strength: Nylon > Silk > Cotton.",
         "Believing natural plant fibres are mechanically stronger than high-performance synthetic polymers.",
         "A chain made of short wooden links slips apart; a single continuous steel cable lifts a bridge.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which thread would you choose to stitch heavy leather shoes or climbing parachutes?",
         "Connect macromolecular structure to empirical tensile strength."),

        ("Chemical test to distinguish animal vs plant fibres", "Outline a chemical laboratory test using dilute Sodium Hydroxide (caustic soda) solution to distinguish between a tuft of pure cotton and a tuft of pure wool.",
         "Student thinks both will dissolve into clear water instantly.",
         "Place samples in warm dilute Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH): Wool (animal protein) dissolves completely, leaving a clear liquid; Cotton (plant cellulose) resists alkali and remains intact without dissolving.",
         "Animal protein fibres (keratin in wool) have disulfide bonds that are hydrolyzed and destroyed by strong alkaline solutions, whereas plant cellulose is chemically stable in alkaline solutions (which is why caustic soda is used in cotton processing).",
         "Step 1: Take two clean test tubes; label Tube A (Cotton) and Tube B (Wool).\nStep 2: Place a 1-gram tuft of cotton in Tube A and a 1-gram tuft of wool in Tube B.\nStep 3: Add 10 mL of 10% Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH / caustic soda) solution to each tube.\nStep 4: Warm both tubes gently in a hot water bath for 5 to 10 minutes.\nStep 5: Observe Tube A: cotton cellulose fibres swell slightly but remain completely undissolved.\nStep 6: Observe Tube B: wool fibres dissolve completely, leaving a yellowish liquid.\nStep 7: Conclude: chemical dissolution in alkali definitively distinguishes protein fibres from cellulose fibres.",
         "Confusing alkaline chemical reactivity of proteins with that of cellulose.",
         "Hot tea dissolves sugar cubes completely, but does not dissolve the metal teaspoon.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What safety equipment (goggles, gloves) must you wear when heating caustic soda in a lab?",
         "Teach disciplined chemical differentiation and laboratory safety."),

        ("Calculating fabric requirements for tailoring", "A tailor needs to stitch 5 identical school shirts. Each shirt requires 1.8 metres of fabric that is 90 cm wide. If the fabric store only sells wider fabric that is 140 cm wide, how does the tailor calculate whether less total length is needed?",
         "Student says fabric width has zero effect on the length needed.",
         "Wider fabric (140 cm) allows shirt pattern pieces (front, back, sleeves) to be laid side-by-side across the width rather than stacked vertically along the length, reducing the total required fabric length to approx. 1.2 to 1.3 metres per shirt.",
         "Tailoring layout efficiency depends on fabric width: wider fabric accommodates multiple garment components within a single length bracket, reducing total yardage needed and minimizing cut scrap waste.",
         "Step 1: Calculate total surface area needed per shirt: approx. 1.8 m × 0.9 m = 1.62 square metres.\nStep 2: Examine narrow fabric (90 cm): sleeve pieces cannot fit next to the torso pieces across 90 cm; must be placed sequentially along the length.\nStep 3: Examine wide fabric (140 cm): torso panels and sleeve panels fit side-by-side across the 140 cm width.\nStep 4: Calculate length savings: each shirt requires only ~1.25 m of 140 cm fabric instead of 1.8 m of 90 cm fabric.\nStep 5: Multiply for 5 shirts: 5 × 1.25 m = 6.25 metres total (saving nearly 2.75 metres of fabric).",
         "Assuming fabric purchasing is measured purely in linear metres regardless of roll width.",
         "Packing books in a narrow box forces you to stack them high; packing in a wide box lets you lay them side-by-side.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a cutting layout diagram comparing pattern pieces arranged on 90 cm fabric versus 140 cm fabric.",
         "Connect 2D geometric tessellation and spatial planning to everyday tailoring mathematics.")
    ]

