    # 66..80: Diagram, graph, or virtual-lab reasoning (15)
    diag_data = [
        ("Diagram: Warp and Weft on a handloom", "Examine the diagram of a simple handloom. Label the longitudinal stretched threads, the shuttle carrying the crosswise thread, and explain how the shuttle moves.",
         "Student thinks the shuttle is a space rocket launching into the sky.",
         "The longitudinal taut threads are the warp; the moving device is the shuttle carrying the weft thread horizontally through the separated shed of warp yarns.",
         "A loom separates alternating warp threads into an upper and lower layer (creating a shed). The shuttle slides through this gap, laying a single weft yarn across, which is then beaten tight by the reed comb.",
         "1. Observe diagram: longitudinal vertical parallel yarns = Warp.\n2. Observe boat-shaped tool = Shuttle (holds a bobbin of weft yarn).\n3. Shed mechanism: foot pedals raise alternating warp threads, opening a triangular tunnel.\n4. Shuttle transit: weaver throws the shuttle through the tunnel from left to right.\n5. Battening: reed comb presses the new weft row tightly against the finished cloth edge.",
         "Failing to understand the mechanics of loom shed creation and shuttle passage.",
         "A train passing through a railway tunnel: the tunnel is the open shed of warp yarns, and the train is the shuttle laying track.",
         "Diagram", "What tool on the loom beats the newly placed weft yarn tight against the finished fabric?",
         "Teach loom anatomy using clear mechanical schematic diagrams."),

        ("Diagram: Microscopic view of natural vs synthetic fibres", "Look at the three microscopic cross-section drawings: Fibre A is a flat twisted ribbon with a hollow lumen; Fibre B is a scaly cylinder with irregular crimp; Fibre C is a perfectly uniform, smooth solid cylinder. Identify each fibre.",
         "Student thinks all three are just different types of cooked spaghetti.",
         "Fibre A is Cotton (collapsed hollow ribbon of plant cellulose); Fibre B is Wool (scaly animal hair cylinder with cuticle scales); Fibre C is Synthetic Nylon/Polyester (extruded uniform plastic cylinder).",
         "Under a high-power microscope, natural fibres show biological irregularities (twists, lumen cavities, scales), whereas synthetic fibres extruded through engineered spinneret dies are mathematically smooth and uniform.",
         "1. Fibre A: flat, twisted ribbon-like morphology with a central collapsed channel (lumen) -> classic Cotton.\n2. Fibre B: cylindrical shaft covered in overlapping shingles/scales (cuticle) -> classic Wool.\n3. Fibre C: perfectly smooth, glass-like rod with zero surface scales or pits -> extruded Synthetic (nylon/polyester).\n4. Conclude microscopic surface morphology reveals biological vs industrial origin.",
         "Assuming fibres look identical under a microscope.",
         "A fallen tree branch is rugged and twisted (natural); a plastic extrusion pipe is perfectly smooth and round (synthetic).",
         "Diagram", "What are the overlapping shingle-like structures visible on the surface of wool fibres called?",
         "Teach microscopic morphological analysis for textile identification."),

        ("Diagram: Spinning tools - Takli and Charkha", "Compare the diagrams of the Takli and the Charkha. What mechanical advantage does the Charkha provide over the simple Takli?",
         "Student thinks the charkha is just an ornamental museum decoration.",
         "The charkha uses a large belt-driven wheel to multiply rotational speed, spinning the spindle dozens of times faster for each turn of the handle, producing yarn far more rapidly than a hand-rotated takli.",
         "The takli is spun by manual finger twisting and drops under gravity. The charkha incorporates a wheel-and-axle gear ratio: one slow rotation of the large drive wheel turns the tiny spindle pulley 30 to 40 times.",
         "1. Takli mechanism: vertical weighted spindle spun by rolling between fingers or along the thigh (slow, intermittent).\n2. Charkha mechanism: large hand-cranked wheel connected by a driving cord to a tiny spindle whorl.\n3. Gear ratio: large circumference driving small circumference creates high angular velocity.\n4. Productivity: increases yarn output tenfold while providing uniform drafting tension.",
         "Viewing ancient tools as crude without appreciating simple machine physics (wheel and axle).",
         "A bicycle pedal: turning the large gear once turns the back bicycle wheel multiple times.",
         "Diagram", "Identify the driving cord that connects the large wheel to the spindle on a charkha.",
         "Integrate simple machine physics (wheel-and-axle ratio) into textile history."),

        ("Graph: Tensile strength of different fibre yarns", "A bar graph shows breaking force (in Newtons) for equal-thickness yarns: Jute = 15 N, Cotton = 20 N, Silk = 45 N, Nylon = 95 N. Which fibre has the highest tensile strength, and how many times stronger is nylon than cotton?",
         "Student misreads the graph and says jute is the strongest because it is used for ropes.",
         "Nylon has the highest tensile strength (95 N); nylon is 4.75 times stronger than cotton (95 N ÷ 20 N = 4.75).",
         "Synthetic nylon polymers have extremely long, aligned molecular chains that resist immense pulling forces before snapping, vastly outperforming natural plant cellulose fibres of identical diameter.",
         "1. Read bar heights: Jute = 15 N; Cotton = 20 N; Silk = 45 N; Nylon = 95 N.\n2. Identify maximum bar: Nylon at 95 N.\n3. Calculate ratio: Nylon breaking load ÷ Cotton breaking load = 95 ÷ 20 = 4.75.\n4. Conclude nylon is nearly five times stronger than cotton under tension.",
         "Confusing rope bulk with material tensile strength.",
         "A thick rope of dry grass breaks easily; a thin wire of steel holds a crane.",
         "Graph", "Which natural fibre in that graph ranks highest in breaking strength?",
         "Build quantitative bar graph interpretation skills in physical materials testing."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Simulating fabric burning test", "In a virtual chemistry lab, an unknown fabric thread is touched to a virtual flame: it does not melt; it burns with a steady yellow flame; smoke smells like burning paper; and the residue is a fine, soft grey ash. Identify the fibre.",
         "Student guesses nylon because it burned with a yellow flame.",
         "The unknown fibre is Cotton (or another plant cellulose fibre like linen or rayon); plant cellulose burns with a paper-like smell and leaves soft powdery grey ash without melting.",
         "Paper is made from plant wood cellulose; cotton is nearly 95% pure plant cellulose. Because their chemical composition is identical, they produce identical combustion products and paper-like burning odors.",
         "1. Melting behavior: does not melt or shrink away from heat -> rules out all synthetics (nylon/polyester).\n2. Flame characteristics: burns with steady yellow flame and continues burning after removal.\n3. Odour analysis: smells like burning paper/leaves -> confirms cellulose polymer.\n4. Residue check: soft, light grey-black ash that crushes to powder -> confirms natural plant fibre (Cotton).",
         "Assuming burning with a flame indicates a synthetic plastic material.",
         "Burning a page from a notebook smells identical to burning a cotton ball because both are pure cellulose.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What odour would you expect if the virtual thread had been 100% pure wool?",
         "Reinforce deductive chemical classification using virtual flame test indicators."),

        ("Diagram: Lifecycle of the Silkworm (Bombyx mori)", "Look at the circular lifecycle diagram of the silkworm: Egg -> Larva (Caterpillar) -> Pupa inside Cocoon -> Adult Silk Moth. At which specific stage is the commercial silk harvested?",
         "Student picks the adult flying moth stage.",
         "Commercial silk is harvested at the Cocoon (Pupa) stage, before the pupa transforms into an adult moth and breaks the continuous silk thread to escape.",
         "The silkworm caterpillar builds the cocoon to protect itself during metamorphosis. To reel continuous silk filament, farmers must harvest the cocoons before the emerging adult moth cuts the fibres.",
         "1. Stage 1 (Egg): female moth lays hundreds of eggs on mulberry paper.\n2. Stage 2 (Larva): caterpillars eat mulberry leaves for 4-6 weeks, molting 4 times.\n3. Stage 3 (Cocoon): mature caterpillar spins a dense silk cocoon around itself to become a pupa.\n4. Commercial harvest: cocoons are collected and sorted at this exact pupal stage.\n5. Stage 4 (Adult): if unharvested, moth secretes enzyme, dissolves cocoon wall, and emerges with wings.",
         "Believing silk is collected from the adult moth's wings or body.",
         "Harvesting a cocoon is like picking a ripe fruit before the seeds drop to the ground.",
         "Diagram", "What plant leaves do silkworm caterpillars feed on exclusively during their larval growth?",
         "Teach life-cycle biology and sericultural harvesting intervention points."),

        ("Graph: Moisture regain percentage of textile fibres", "A bar graph shows moisture regain (percentage of water absorbed from humid air at 65% RH): Polyester = 0.4%, Nylon = 4.0%, Cotton = 8.5%, Wool = 16.0%. Why does wool feel comfortable in humid cold weather compared to polyester?",
         "Student thinks wool is comfortable because it pushes all water away like a raincoat.",
         "Wool absorbs up to 16% of its dry weight in moisture vapour directly from humid air into its internal chemical structure without feeling wet to the touch, while releasing small amounts of heat (heat of sorption) that keep you warm.",
         "Wool keratin contains hydrophilic amino acid groups that bind water vapour inside the fibre cortex. As water vapour condenses into the wool fibre, latent heat is released, providing natural warmth.",
         "1. Read bar heights: Polyester absorbs only 0.4% moisture; Wool absorbs 16.0% moisture vapour.\n2. Absorption site: water vapour enters the interior protein cortex, leaving the outer cuticle dry to touch.\n3. Exothermic reaction: chemical binding of water molecules releases heat of sorption (~1 kJ per gram of absorbed water).\n4. Contrast with polyester: zero moisture uptake leaves clammy sweat condensed on skin.",
         "Assuming fabrics that absorb moisture must immediately feel wet, heavy, and cold.",
         "Wool is like a smart clay matka that absorbs moisture while keeping its outer surface dry.",
         "Graph", "Which fibre in that graph absorbs the lowest percentage of moisture vapour from humid air?",
         "Introduce advanced textile moisture dynamics and heat of sorption via bar graphs."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Testing chemical dissolution of fibres", "In a virtual lab, two fabric samples are placed in beakers of cold Acetone (nail polish remover): Sample 1 dissolves completely within 30 seconds; Sample 2 is completely unaffected. Identify Sample 1 from: Cotton, Acetate/Synthetic, Wool.",
         "Student thinks cotton dissolves in nail polish remover.",
         "Sample 1 is Acetate (a semi-synthetic cellulose acetate plastic); Sample 2 is Cotton or Wool, which are insoluble in organic acetone solvent.",
         "Synthetic plastics and cellulose derivatives dissolve in organic solvents like acetone because the solvent breaks their intermolecular polymer bonds. Natural cotton cellulose and wool keratin are completely resistant to acetone.",
         "1. Solvent properties: Acetone is a polar organic solvent used in paint thinners and nail polish removers.\n2. Natural fibre resistance: Cotton (cellulose) and Wool (keratin) do not dissolve in acetone.\n3. Synthetic reactivity: Acetate and certain synthetic polymers dissolve rapidly as acetone breaks ester bonds.\n4. Diagnostic conclusion: instant dissolution in acetone proves the presence of acetate synthetic fibres.",
         "Assuming household nail polish remover dissolves natural cotton cloth.",
         "Water dissolves sugar but cannot dissolve a glass marble; acetone dissolves plastic but leaves cotton unharmed.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What simple safety precaution must you follow when handling volatile flammable acetone in a laboratory?",
         "Teach chemical solvent identification tests and flammability precautions."),

        ("Diagram: Cotton boll anatomy and fibre attachment", "Study the botanical cross-section diagram of an open cotton boll: show the carpel walls, the central placenta, and the seeds covered with single-celled hair fibres. What is each individual cotton fibre biologically?",
         "Student thinks each cotton fibre is a microscopic tree branch.",
         "Biologically, each cotton fibre is a single, immensely elongated epidermal cell that grows outward from the surface of the cotton seed coat (testa).",
         "A single cotton fibre is one of the longest single cells in the plant kingdom, elongating up to 3 to 4 centimetres before its cell wall thickens with pure cellulose and its living protoplasm dies, leaving a hollow ribbon.",
         "1. Origin: outer epidermal layer of the developing seed coat.\n2. Cellular nature: a single living plant cell that elongates thousands of times its width.\n3. Secondary wall thickening: layers of crystalline cellulose deposit inside the cell membrane.\n4. Maturation: upon boll bursting, the cell dries out, the central sap cavity (lumen) collapses, and the cell twists into a flat convolute ribbon.",
         "Believing a cotton fibre is a multicellular stem or vein.",
         "A hair growing from your scalp is a strand of protein; a cotton fibre is a single plant cell growing from a seed.",
         "Diagram", "What happens to the liquid-filled central lumen when the cotton boll bursts open to the dry air?",
         "Teach cellular biology and morphological maturation of seed hairs."),

        ("Graph: Global fibre production breakdown", "A pie chart shows global annual fibre production: Synthetic fibres (Polyester/Nylon) = 64%, Cotton = 24%, Other Plant fibres (Jute/Flax) = 6%, Animal fibres (Wool/Silk) = 2%, Cellulosic (Rayon) = 4%. What proportion of the world's clothing is made from synthetic petroleum fibres?",
         "Student says almost 100% of world clothing is made from natural cotton.",
         "Nearly two-thirds (64%) of all fibres produced globally are synthetic petroleum-derived fibres, with polyester being the single most produced fibre on Earth.",
         "While natural fibres dominated history, industrial chemistry since 1950 has caused synthetic fibres to overtake natural fibres due to low cost, rapid manufacturing, and continuous filament production.",
         "1. Read pie chart sectors: Synthetics = 64%; Cotton = 24%; Others = 12% combined.\n2. Dominant category: Synthetic polymers derived from crude oil and petrochemicals.\n3. Economic driver: polyester is cheaper to manufacture in chemical reactors than farming cotton or rearing sheep.\n4. Environmental challenge: 64% of global textiles are non-biodegradable and contribute to persistent microplastic pollution.",
         "Assuming modern society still wears predominantly natural agricultural fabrics.",
         "Looking at a parking lot where two out of every three cars are plastic-bodied electric scooters.",
         "Graph", "What percentage of global fibre production is represented by animal fibres (wool and silk) combined?",
         "Build global economic and industrial awareness regarding synthetic dominance."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Simulating yarn spinning twist (Twist multiplier)", "In a virtual spinning simulator, a student tests yarn breaking strength as a function of twists per centimetre: 0 twists = 0 N (pulls apart like cloud), 5 twists = 8 N, 15 twists = 28 N, 30 twists = 35 N, 60 twists = 12 N (yarn kinks and snaps). What causes yarn strength to drop when over-twisted?",
         "Student thinks twisting a yarn infinitely makes it infinitely strong.",
         "Optimal twisting increases frictional grip between fibres, maximizing strength; but excessive over-twisting introduces high internal torsional stress and shearing, causing the yarn to kink, buckle, and snap under lower tension.",
         "Spinning physics requires a sweet spot: too little twist allows fibres to slip apart without friction; too much twist shears the fibres and kinks the thread into snarls that fracture easily.",
         "1. Low twist regime (0-5 twists/cm): fibres are parallel; slip past each other under tiny forces.\n2. Optimal twist regime (15-30 twists/cm): lateral pressure forces fibres tightly together, maximizing friction without damage.\n3. Over-twist regime (40-60 twists/cm): severe torsional shear stress breaks individual fibres; yarn coils into corkscrew snarls.\n4. Conclude spinning requires precise calculation of the optimal twist multiplier.",
         "Believing more twist always produces more strength indefinitely.",
         "Wringing a wet towel: twisting squeezes water out, but twisting with giant pliers tears the cloth to shreds.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What happens to the appearance of a sewing thread when it is twisted far beyond its breaking limit?",
         "Teach the physics of friction, torsion, and optimal engineering thresholds in spinning."),

        ("Diagram: Cotton Ginning machine cross-section", "Examine the mechanical diagram of Eli Whitney's saw gin: circular rotating saw blades pass through narrow grid slots, carrying cotton fibres through while blocking the seeds. How does the grid slot width separate seeds from fibres?",
         "Student thinks the saw blades crush the seeds into tiny powder.",
         "The slots between the grid ribs are narrower than the diameter of cotton seeds, so seeds are physically blocked and roll down, while the rotating saw teeth hook the soft fibres and pull them through the narrow slots.",
         "The saw gin is a brilliant mechanical size-exclusion filter: hooked metal teeth pull flexible lint through narrow metal combs while rigid seeds are blocked and tumble into a collection chute.",
         "1. Components: hopper box, rotating saw cylinder with curved teeth, slotted grid ribs, rotary brush cylinder.\n2. Size-exclusion principle: width of grid slots is ~3 mm; cotton seeds are 5-8 mm in diameter.\n3. Fibre capture: hooked saw teeth grab fluffy cotton lint and pull it through the 3 mm slot.\n4. Seed separation: rigid seeds cannot pass through the narrow slot and fall into the seed hopper below.\n5. Doffing: a high-speed rotating brush strips clean cotton lint from the saw teeth.",
         "Assuming the machine crushes the seeds rather than separating them intact.",
         "A coin sorting machine where small coins fall through holes and large coins slide across the top.",
         "Diagram", "What tool on the ginning machine brushes the clean cotton lint off the rotating saw teeth?",
         "Teach mechanical engineering principles and historical agricultural technology."),

        ("Graph: Water absorption over time in different weaves", "A line graph tracks water absorption rate (seconds to absorb a water drop): Plain weave cotton = 3 sec, Twill weave denim = 18 sec, Coated canvas = 600 sec (beaded). Why does plain weave absorb water drops six times faster than twill denim?",
         "Student thinks plain weave cloth is made of magic sponge threads.",
         "Plain weave has a looser, more open structure with accessible capillary spaces between yarns that allow water to penetrate instantly; tightly packed diagonal twill weave has dense yarns that slow capillary liquid ingress.",
         "Weave density dictates liquid penetration speed: open weaves provide rapid capillary wicking, whereas dense, tightly beaten twill weaves resist fluid penetration.",
         "1. Plain weave structure: 1-over-1-under grid leaves small open pores between interlacing points.\n2. Twill weave structure: 2-over-1-under or 3-over-1-under with heavy yarns packed tightly under high loom beating.\n3. Capillary infiltration: water droplets spread rapidly along open pores in plain weave (3 seconds).\n4. In twill denim: compact yarn density resists droplet wetting for 18 seconds.\n5. Coated canvas: hydrophobic coating prevents wetting completely (600+ seconds).",
         "Ignoring the profound effect of fabric weave density on liquid penetration speed.",
         "Water flows instantly through a wire garden sieve, but drips very slowly through tightly packed sand.",
         "Graph", "Which fabric weave in that graph would be superior for making an absorbent kitchen dish towel?",
         "Connect fabric weave geometry to everyday household functionality."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Identifying knitted vs woven structures under zoom", "In a virtual microscope, a student examines two mystery fabric samples at 50x magnification: Sample X shows continuous interconnected serpentine loops chaining vertically; Sample Y shows two sets of straight perpendicular yarns crossing over and under. Classify X and Y.",
         "Student classifies Sample X as woven because it has threads.",
         "Sample X is a Knitted fabric (interlocking loops); Sample Y is a Woven fabric (criss-cross warp and weft grid).",
         "Microscopic structural geometry is the definitive diagnostic key: knitted fabrics consist of looped chains (wales and courses), while woven fabrics consist of a rigid orthogonal grid (warp and weft).",
         "1. Observe Sample X at 50x: single continuous yarn curved into interlocking loops like chains of stitches -> Knitting.\n2. Functional correlation for X: loops flex and slide, giving high elasticity (e.g. sweater, sock, T-shirt).\n3. Observe Sample Y at 50x: rigid straight yarns running in two perpendicular directions (vertical warp, horizontal weft) -> Weaving.\n4. Functional correlation for Y: interlocking grid prevents stretch, providing dimensional stability (e.g. jeans, bedsheet).",
         "Relying on fabric feel rather than microscopic structural loop-versus-grid architecture.",
         "Looking at chain mail armor (interlocked rings like knitting) versus a wire mesh screen (crossed straight wires like weaving).",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which sample (X or Y) would unravel completely if you pulled a single snagged loop?",
         "Establish visual diagnostic mastery of knitting versus weaving architectures."),

        ("Diagram: Retting tank bacterial decomposition", "Examine a biological diagram of a jute retting tank: bundles of green jute stalks submerged in water, showing anaerobic bacteria attacking the pectin middle lamella between plant cell walls. What chemical substance are the bacteria digesting?",
         "Student thinks the bacteria are eating the cellulose fibre itself.",
         "The bacteria are digesting pectin and hemicellulose gums that cement the fibres to the woody stem; they do not digest the strong cellulose fibres if retting is stopped at the correct time.",
         "Plant stems use calcium pectate (pectin) as biological glue to bind vascular fibres to the woody core. Retting bacteria secrete pectinase enzymes that dissolve this glue, leaving pure insoluble cellulose fibres intact.",
         "1. Anatomy of jute stalk: outer bark, phloem fibre bundles, pectin glue (middle lamella), inner woody hurd.\n2. Bacterial action: anaerobic microbes (e.g. Clostridium) produce pectinolytic enzymes.\n3. Selective digestion: pectinase dissolves the sticky pectin cement without hydrolyzing cellulose.\n4. Separation: once pectin dissolves, fibre bundles detach freely from the woody stem.\n5. Over-retting risk: if left too long, cellulase-producing bacteria attack and ruin the cellulose fibres.",
         "Assuming retting bacteria consume the valuable fibre rather than the unwanted pectin glue.",
         "Soaking a postage stamp in water dissolves the sticky gum without dissolving the paper stamp.",
         "Diagram", "What dangerous enzyme would destroy the actual jute fibres if retting is continued for too long?",
         "Teach selective microbial enzymatic digestion in industrial agro-processing.")
    ]

